# <%= it.displayName %> — Data Formats Site

An Astro + [Starlight](https://starlight.astro.build) docs site for the
<%= it.displayName %> reverse-engineering project. It presents curated,
human-readable pages alongside searchable galleries of the game's extracted
sprite/icon atlases, self-contained enough to deploy on its own (see
`.github/workflows/deploy.yml`).

## Getting started

```bash
npm install
npm run dev
```

`npm run dev`/`npm run build`/`npm run start` all run `node scripts/build.mjs`
first, which does two things (see the comment at the top of that file):

1. copies `<repo-root>/public/assets/<%= it.game %>` into this site's own
   `public/assets/<%= it.game %>`;
2. regenerates `generated/sidebar.mjs` from
   `src/content/docs/<%= it.game %>/_sidebar.json`.

## The asset-copy-and-fallback convention

`scripts/build.mjs` prefers the repo's live `public/assets/<%= it.game %>`
(the output of the extraction pipeline) when it exists, and falls back to
whatever is already committed under this site's own `public/assets/<%= it.game %>`
when it doesn't. **Commit the site's copy of `public/assets/<%= it.game %>`
even though the raw extraction output usually isn't committed at the repo
root** — CI checks out this repo alone, never the original copyrighted game
files, so it has no way to regenerate the assets from scratch. Committing the
site's mirror is what lets CI build and deploy without ever touching the
original data.

When the repo's extracted assets change, re-run `npm run dev` or
`npm run build` here to refresh the mirror, then commit whatever changed
under `public/assets/<%= it.game %>`.

## Adding a new curated page

1. Add a `.md` or `.mdx` file under `src/content/docs/<%= it.game %>/`.
2. Add a matching sidebar entry to
   `src/content/docs/<%= it.game %>/_sidebar.json` — either a top-level
   `{ "label", "slug" }` entry, or nested inside a group's `"items"` array.
   Slugs are content file paths under `src/content/docs/` (the game's home
   page slug is `<%= it.game %>`, not `<%= it.game %>/index`).
3. Re-run the dev server or build — the sidebar is regenerated from that
   manifest on every run, never hand-edit `generated/sidebar.mjs`.

## `SpriteGallery`

`src/components/SpriteGallery.astro` renders a searchable grid from a
packed-frames sprite atlas — the same `{ frames: [{ name, x, y, w, h }],
width, height }` shape used by the offline asset viewer
(`create-seer-viewer`). Given `manifest="items"` and
`assetPath="<%= it.game %>/amiga/sprites"`, it reads
`public/assets/<%= it.game %>/amiga/sprites/items.json` and `.png` — `assetPath`
is the *complete* directory containing them, so if your project doesn't keep
atlases in their own `sprites/` subfolder, just omit that segment
(`assetPath="<%= it.game %>/amiga"`). This component has no opinion on your
project's own layout convention.

```mdx
import SpriteGallery from '../../../components/SpriteGallery.astro';

<SpriteGallery
  manifest="items"
  assetPath="<%= it.game %>/amiga/sprites"
  label="item"
  hoverPairs={{ item010: 'item011' }}
  clickChains={{ item020: ['item020', 'item021', 'item022'] }}
  labels={new Map([['item010', { visible: 'Fire Wand', hover: 'Fire Wand (lit)' }]])}
/>
```

- `hoverPairs` — frame name → frame name. Cards for the first frame swap to
  the second on hover (e.g. a dim/activated icon pair). Omit entirely if the
  manifest has none.
- `clickChains` — frame name → ordered list of frame names. The card starts
  on the first frame and advances one step per click (mouse or
  Enter/Space), latching on the last frame. Omit for no click behavior.
- `labels` — a `Map<frameName, { visible, hover? }>` built by the *calling
  page*, however it wants (a static object, a JSON file it reads itself,
  whatever the game's name data actually looks like). The component has no
  built-in knowledge of any name-lookup file format — it only ever calls
  `labels.get(frame.name)` and falls back to the raw frame name.

## Pointing the favicon at a real sprite

`scripts/build.mjs` calls `scripts/generate_favicon.mjs <atlasDir> <manifest>
<frameName> <output>` to extract one sprite frame as `public/favicon.png`.
Until you configure it, the build skips this step and uses the placeholder
`public/favicon.png` shipped by this scaffold. To point it at a real sprite:

1. Open `scripts/build.mjs` and set `FAVICON_FRAME` to the frame's `name`
   (e.g. `"item042"`), and `FAVICON_ATLAS_DIR`/`FAVICON_MANIFEST` to the
   sprite sheet it lives in (e.g. `"amiga/sprites"` and `"items.json"`).
2. Re-run `npm run build` — it regenerates `public/favicon.png` from that
   frame and updates `astro.config.mjs`'s `favicon` reference automatically
   (it always points at `/favicon.png`).

## Deploying

`.github/workflows/deploy.yml` builds and deploys this site with
[withastro/action](https://github.com/withastro/action) whenever `main` is
pushed. It's already wired to this site's directory — no changes needed
beyond enabling GitHub Pages for the repo.
